{"title":"DynaKRAG: A Unified Framework for Learnable Evidence Control in Multi-Hop Retrieval-Augmented Generation","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.06507","arxiv_id":"2607.06507","authors":["Yaqi Wu","Xiaolei Guo","Chenyu Zhou","Jiaqi Huang","Xianfa Zhang","Junxu Zhang","Zhuo Yu","Zhubo Shi","Jianghao Lin","Dongdong Ge"],"year":2026,"published":"2026-07-07","abstract":"DynaKRAG formulates multi-hop evidence acquisition as state-conditioned control over atomic evidence operations. Its shared evidence state records retrieved documents, frontier information, query/action history, bridge candidates, and diagnostic feedback; a learned controller can choose retrieval, query rewriting, bridge expansion, gap-directed retrieval, sufficiency checking, or stopping. Controlled experiments show that sufficiency feedback and learned operation selection materially improve multi-hop QA, while additional retrieval is not uniformly beneficial."}
{"title":"FAIR-RAG: Faithful Adaptive Iterative Refinement for Retrieval-Augmented Generation","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22344","arxiv_id":"2510.22344","authors":["Mohammad Aghajani Asl","Majid Asgari-Bidhendi","Behrooz Minaei-Bidgoli"],"year":2025,"published":"2025-10-25","abstract":"FAIR-RAG uses a Structured Evidence Assessment module that decomposes a question into a checklist of required findings, audits accumulated evidence, and explicitly records confirmed facts and unresolved information gaps. Gap items drive targeted follow-up queries, and the loop repeats until the evidence is judged sufficient for faithful generation. It is evaluated on HotpotQA, 2WikiMultiHopQA, and MuSiQue."}
{"title":"AdaGATE: Adaptive Gap-Aware Token-Efficient Evidence Assembly for Multi-Hop Retrieval-Augmented Generation","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.05245","arxiv_id":"2605.05245","authors":["Yilin Guo","Yinshan Wang","Yixuan Wang"],"year":2026,"published":"2026-05-04","abstract":"AdaGATE is a training-free evidence controller that treats multi-hop evidence acquisition as a token-constrained repair problem. It tracks entity-centric evidence gaps, generates targeted micro-queries, and scores candidates using gap coverage, corroboration, novelty, redundancy, and direct relevance while replacing low-utility evidence under a fixed token budget. Experiments on HotpotQA under clean, redundant, and noisy retrieval conditions evaluate evidence quality as well as token efficiency."}
{"title":"AdaKG-RAG: Adaptive KG-guided retrieval-augmented generation with hypothesis-and-verification retrieval for multi-hop question answering","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s44443-026-01028-3","doi":"10.1007/s44443-026-01028-3","authors":["Mengsi Yuan","Tao Wang","Yurong Qian"],"year":2026,"published":"2026-07-11","abstract":"AdaKG-RAG centers multi-hop retrieval on a Hypothesis-and-Verification Retrieval loop. The system forms a tentative answer from current evidence, verifies whether the evidence is sufficient, and triggers targeted retrieval refinement when bridge facts are missing; graph expansion and path-aware evidence organization support the loop. Budget-matched ablations distinguish gains from evidence-sufficiency feedback from gains caused merely by additional retrieval rounds or context.","venue":"Journal of King Saud University Computer and Information Sciences"}
{"title":"MEGRAG: Multi-Granular Evidence Graphs for Answer-Aware Multi-Hop RAG","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.02195","arxiv_id":"2608.02195","authors":["Weidong Bao","Yingying Sun","Jun Yang","Yilin Wang","Zili Wei","Yubin Bao","Fangling Leng","Minghe Yu","Tiancheng Zhang","Ge Yu"],"year":2026,"published":"2026-08-03","abstract":"MEGRAG represents multi-hop reasoning as a path-structured, multi-granular evidence graph linking passages, sentences, and extracted triples. At each stage it uses an intermediate answer and prior reasoning to decide whether the original query has been resolved; unresolved cases produce an explicit missing-information diagnosis and focused next query, while resolved cases stop retrieval. This directly couples structured evidence representation, gap identification, iterative retrieval, and semantic stopping."}
{"title":"ECoRAG: Evidentiality-guided Compression for Long Context RAG","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.05167","arxiv_id":"2506.05167","authors":["Yeonseok Jeong","Jinsu Kim","Dohyeon Lee","Seung-won Hwang"],"year":2025,"published":"2025-06-05","abstract":"ECoRAG compresses retrieved documents according to evidentiality rather than general relevance alone. After compression it explicitly assesses whether the retained content supplies sufficient evidence and retrieves more when it does not, continuing until sufficiency is reached. Experiments on open-domain QA also evaluate latency and token-use savings.","venue":"Findings of the Association for Computational Linguistics: ACL 2025"}
{"title":"Knowing You Don't Know: Learning When to Continue Search in Multi-round RAG through Self-Practicing","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.02811","arxiv_id":"2505.02811","doi":"10.1145/3726302.3730018","authors":["Diji Yang","Linda Zeng","Jinmeng Rao","Yi Zhang"],"year":2025,"published":"2025-07-13","abstract":"SIM-RAG trains a lightweight information-sufficiency critic from synthetic multi-round retrieval trajectories generated through self-practice. At inference time the critic evaluates after each round whether enough information has been retrieved and guides the decision to continue searching or stop. The method is evaluated across multiple RAG benchmarks without requiring human-labeled intermediate retrieval supervision.","venue":"SIGIR 2025"}
{"title":"A Reproducibility Study of Metacognitive Retrieval-Augmented Generation","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.19899","arxiv_id":"2604.19899","doi":"10.1145/3805712.3808551","authors":["Gabriel Iturra-Bocaz","Petra Galuščáková"],"year":2026,"published":"2026-07-19","abstract":"This study reproduces MetaRAG, a multi-retrieval framework intended to recognize when enough information has been gathered, and compares it with SIM-RAG's lightweight sufficiency critic. It confirms relative improvements but reports lower absolute results than the original MetaRAG study and examines sensitivity to reranking and sufficiency judgments. The work is useful evidence on the reproducibility and robustness of semantic stopping mechanisms.","venue":"SIGIR 2026"}
{"title":"RMIT-ADM+S at the MMU-RAG NeurIPS 2025 Competition","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.20735","arxiv_id":"2602.20735","authors":["Kun Ran","Marwah Alaofi","Danula Hettiachchi","Chenglong Ma","Khoi Nguyen Dinh Anh","Khoi Vo Nguyen","Sachin Pathiyan Cherumanal","Lida Rashidi","Falk Scholer","Damiano Spina","Shuoqi Sun","Oleg Zendel"],"year":2026,"published":"2026-02-24","abstract":"The paper presents R2RAG, which dynamically adapts retrieval strategy using query complexity and evidence sufficiency. For iterative cases, accumulated evidence is reviewed for coverage of query subparts and remaining knowledge gaps; further queries target those gaps, while stopping also respects a context-token threshold and a bounded iteration cap. The system was evaluated in the MMU-RAG competition's dynamic setting.","venue":"NeurIPS 2025 MMU-RAG Competition Workshop"}
{"title":"Beyond Relevance: On the Relationship Between Retrieval and RAG Information Coverage","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.08819","arxiv_id":"2603.08819","doi":"10.1145/3805713.3820424","authors":["Saron Samuel","Alexander Martin","Eugene Yang","Andrew Yates","Dawn Lawrie","Ian Soboroff","Laura Dietz","Benjamin Van Durme"],"year":2026,"published":"2026-07-25","abstract":"This work studies whether upstream retrieval measurements predict the information coverage of downstream RAG answers. Across text and multimodal RAG benchmarks, coverage-oriented retrieval metrics correlate more strongly with generated-response coverage than ordinary relevance metrics when the task requires broad information coverage. The results also show that iterative RAG can partially decouple final generation quality from initial retrieval effectiveness.","venue":"ICTIR 2026"}
{"title":"Budgeted Multi-Hop Retrieval Agent for Compositional Question Answering: A Retrieval-Policy Evaluation on the Official MultiHop-RAG Benchmark","url":"https://doi.org/10.51903/jtie.v4i3.543","doi":"10.51903/jtie.v4i3.543","authors":["Wenhao Su","Siyu Chen","Chloe Zhao"],"year":2025,"published":"2025-12-20","abstract":"The paper evaluates a budgeted multi-hop retrieval agent on questions whose evidence spans multiple documents and combines source, time, comparison, and entity constraints. Evaluation jointly reports answer quality, evidence recall, retrieval rounds, selected documents, and context-token cost, rather than measuring compute alone. Results expose a cost-versus-evidence-completeness trade-off and motivate hop-aware or dynamic budget allocation.","venue":"Journal of Technology Informatics and Engineering"}
{"title":"AdaRAG: Budget-Aware Adaptive Retrieval-Augmented Generation via Hierarchical Reinforcement Learning","url":"https://doi.org/10.1109/jcc72984.2026.00024","doi":"10.1109/jcc72984.2026.00024","authors":["J. Sun","M. Zhang","C. Chen","H. Wei","L. Yin","C. Ai","B. Yin"],"year":2026,"abstract":"AdaRAG treats multi-turn retrieval as a budget-constrained control problem. Under a hard document budget, it learns how many retrieval rounds to perform, how many documents to retrieve in each round, and which retrieval source to use, rather than fixing Top-K independently of query structure.","venue":"2026 IEEE International Conference on Joint Cloud Computing"}
{"title":"When Knowledge Is Not Free: Cost-Aware Evidence Selection in Retrieval-Augmented Generation","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.02245","arxiv_id":"2606.02245","authors":["Mingyan Wu","Han Yang","Omer Ben-Porat","Yftah Ziser"],"year":2026,"published":"2026-06-01","abstract":"This paper introduces cost-aware RAG, in which evidence sources have explicit access-cost tiers and answering must remain within an evidence-access budget. It evaluates static evidence selection and an agentic controller that decides whether to retrieve, which cost tier to access, and when to stop. Results show that larger budgets do not automatically improve answers and that adaptive acquisition behavior is strongly model- and task-dependent."}
{"title":"Time Present and Time Past: Benchmarking Large Language Models on Temporally Evolving Document Understanding","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.08512","arxiv_id":"2608.08512","authors":["Mahbub E Sobhani","Md. Faiyaz Abdullah Sayeedi","Fahmid Hasan Chowdhury","Md Adnan Arefeen","Farig Sadeque","Md. Faizul Bari","Swakkhar Shatabda"],"year":2026,"published":"2026-08-09","abstract":"The paper introduces TIDE for question answering over documents that are amended, replaced, and sometimes reverted. Its central task is version resolution: identifying which official version was valid on the queried date, including rejecting a supplied version that did not govern that date. The benchmark contains explicit historical validity periods and evaluates parametric, gold-context, and retrieval-access settings with a hard temporal correctness gate."}
{"title":"SentryLine: Evidence-Grounded Question Answering over Evolving Documents in Oncology Care","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.08364","arxiv_id":"2609.08364","authors":["Tampu Ravi Kumar","Gaurav Najpande","Muhammad Ali Khan","Kaneez Zahra Rubab Khakwani","Karan Kathuria","Shorya Azriel Moses","Yuvraj Kalia","M Bassam Sonbol","Irbaz Bin Riaz","Vivek Gupta"],"year":2026,"published":"2026-09-08","abstract":"SENTRYLINE targets question answering over living oncology guidelines whose recommendations change across versioned documents. Its hierarchical RAG pipeline returns inline evidence citations together with factual and temporal verification reports and drift notes that identify guideline updates. ASCOBENCH evaluates multi-turn questions against expert-authored answers over evolving guideline evidence."}
{"title":"Temporal Validity in Retrieval Memory: Eliminating Stale-Fact Errors for AI Agents over Evolving Knowledge","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.26511","arxiv_id":"2606.26511","authors":["Neeraj Yadav"],"year":2026,"published":"2026-06-25","abstract":"The paper argues that embedding similarity alone cannot reliably distinguish superseded facts from current ones. MemStrata maintains temporal validity and fact supersession in a bi-temporal ledger, retiring contradicted stale values while retaining historical information. It directly addresses lineage and stale-evidence control for retrieval over evolving knowledge."}
{"title":"IDCR: Information-Directed Conformal Retrieval","url":"https://proceedings.mlr.press/v337/nanivadekar26a.html","authors":["Manas Nanivadekar","Jatin Khanijoan","Swayam Kothekar","Mohd Amaan Khan"],"year":2026,"abstract":"IDCR selects evidence according to its effect on downstream conformal uncertainty rather than semantic similarity alone. It minimizes conformal prediction-set volume while preserving distribution-free coverage, derives a monotone submodular retrieval objective, and uses a marginal-gain-separation gate to route uncertain retrieval steps to lookahead search. This provides a direct mechanism for risk-aware evidence acquisition, although it is not an iterative free-form RAG stopping guarantee.","venue":"UAI 2026"}
{"title":"C-RAG: Certified Generation Risks for Retrieval-Augmented Language Models","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.03181","arxiv_id":"2402.03181","authors":["Mintong Kang","Nezihe Merve Gürel","Ning Yu","Dawn Song","Bo Li"],"year":2024,"abstract":"C-RAG develops conformal risk analysis for retrieval-augmented language models and certifies upper confidence bounds for bounded generation-risk functions. Its theory explicitly studies test-distribution shift and gives sufficient conditions under which RAG has lower conformal generation risk than a standalone language model. Retrieval depth itself remains fixed, so the result is most directly transferable as a risk-control layer for adaptive stopping.","venue":"ICML 2024"}
{"title":"TRAQ: Trustworthy Retrieval Augmented Question Answering via Conformal Prediction","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2307.04642","arxiv_id":"2307.04642","doi":"10.18653/v1/2024.naacl-long.210","authors":["Shuo Li","Sangdon Park","Insup Lee","Osbert Bastani"],"year":2024,"abstract":"TRAQ applies conformal prediction to retrieval-augmented open-domain QA and constructs prediction sets guaranteed to contain a semantically correct response with high probability. Bayesian optimization is used to reduce prediction-set size while preserving the statistical correctness target. The guarantee is end-to-end for QA but does not control iterative retrieval depth.","venue":"NAACL 2024"}
{"title":"Response Quality Assessment for Retrieval-Augmented Generation via Conditional Conformal Factuality","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.20978","arxiv_id":"2506.20978","doi":"10.1145/3726302.3730244","authors":["Naihe Feng","Yi Sui","Shiyi Hou","Jesse C. Cresswell","Ga Wu"],"year":2025,"published":"2025-06-26","abstract":"Conformal-RAG applies conformal prediction to quality assessment and filtering of subclaims in RAG responses. It provides group-conditional coverage across multiple subdomains without requiring manually constructed conformal sets and uses internal RAG information to retain more high-quality claims at a fixed reliability target. The method controls final response quality rather than retrieval stopping and is therefore transfer evidence for calibrated risk control.","venue":"SIGIR 2025"}
{"title":"URAG: A Benchmark for Uncertainty Quantification in Retrieval-Augmented Large Language Models","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.19281","arxiv_id":"2603.19281","authors":["Vinh Nguyen","Cuong Dang","Jiahao Zhang","Hoa Tran","Minh Tran","Trinh Chau","Thai Le","Lu Cheng","Suhang Wang"],"year":2026,"published":"2026-03-02","abstract":"URAG benchmarks uncertainty quantification for retrieval-augmented language models across domains and multiple RAG methods, including conformal-prediction-based approaches. Its experiments vary retrieval depth, retrieval noise, and other contextual factors that affect confidence reliability. The benchmark is relevant for evaluating whether a stopping confidence signal remains calibrated as retrieval conditions change."}
{"title":"Is Conformal Factuality for RAG-based LLMs Robust? Novel Metrics and Systematic Insights","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.16817","arxiv_id":"2603.16817","authors":["Yi Chen","Daiwei Chen","Sukrut Madhav Chikodikar","Caitlyn Heqi Yin","Ramya Korlakai Vinayak"],"year":2026,"published":"2026-03-17","abstract":"This study systematically evaluates conformal factuality filtering for RAG-based language models across generation, scoring, calibration, robustness, efficiency, and output usefulness. It introduces informativeness-aware metrics because statistically reliable filtering can produce outputs that are insufficiently informative. The work provides transfer evidence about failure modes that a conformally calibrated stopping controller would need to account for."}
{"title":"Towards Trustworthy Knowledge Graph Reasoning: An Uncertainty Aware Perspective","url":"https://doi.org/10.1609/aaai.v39i12.33353","doi":"10.1609/aaai.v39i12.33353","authors":["Bo Ni","Yu Wang","Lu Cheng","Erik Blasch","Tyler Derr"],"year":2025,"published":"2025-04-11","abstract":"UAG introduces conformal uncertainty quantification into multi-step reasoning that combines knowledge graphs and language models. An error-rate control module allocates error rates across components of the multi-step process so the overall system can achieve a predefined coverage target. Although not an iterative document-RAG stopping controller, it is directly relevant to distributing a calibrated risk budget across structured evidence-acquisition and reasoning stages.","venue":"AAAI 2025"}
{"title":"Selective Question Answering under Domain Shift","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2006.09462","arxiv_id":"2006.09462","doi":"10.18653/v1/2020.acl-main.503","authors":["Amita Kamath","Robin Jia","Percy Liang"],"year":2020,"published":"2020-06-16","abstract":"This paper formalizes selective QA under domain shift, where a system must answer as many questions as possible while maintaining a target accuracy on mixtures of in-domain and unseen out-of-domain inputs. It shows that raw model probabilities are poorly calibrated under shift and trains a separate calibrator using source and known out-of-domain behavior to decide when to abstain. The setting is directly transferable to risk-aware RAG stopping and abstention.","venue":"ACL 2020"}
{"title":"Adaptive Conformal Inference Under Distribution Shift","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2106.00170","arxiv_id":"2106.00170","authors":["Isaac Gibbs","Emmanuel Candès"],"year":2021,"published":"2021-06-01","abstract":"Adaptive conformal inference constructs online prediction sets when the data-generating distribution changes over time in an unknown way. It continuously adapts the conformal control parameter and provably achieves the desired long-run coverage frequency without requiring an exchangeable stream. This is foundational transfer work for calibrating a stopping or abstention controller on a non-stationary evidence stream.","venue":"NeurIPS 2021"}
{"title":"Conformal Inference for Online Prediction with Arbitrary Distribution Shifts","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2208.08401","arxiv_id":"2208.08401","authors":["Isaac Gibbs","Emmanuel J. Candès"],"year":2024,"abstract":"This work extends adaptive conformal inference to online settings with arbitrary distribution changes and develops a procedure with small regret over local time intervals. The method adapts to both the size and type of shift by tuning the update step size online, reducing dependence on knowledge of the data-generating dynamics. It provides a relevant theoretical foundation for non-stationary calibrated stopping.","venue":"Journal of Machine Learning Research"}
{"title":"Selective Classification Under Distribution Shifts","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.05160","arxiv_id":"2405.05160","authors":["Hengyue Liang","Le Peng","Ju Sun"],"year":2024,"published":"2024-05-08","abstract":"The paper develops generalized selective classification for deployment data containing in-distribution, covariate-shifted, and label-shifted or out-of-distribution samples. It studies confidence functions for deciding which cases should be rejected so that error on the retained predictions is controlled more reliably under shift. The framework is transfer evidence for abstention and risk-coverage evaluation of RAG controllers.","venue":"Transactions on Machine Learning Research"}
{"title":"High Probability Risk Control Under Covariate Shift","url":"https://proceedings.mlr.press/v266/almeida25a.html","authors":["Duarte C. Almeida","João Bravo","Jacopo Bono","Pedro Bizarro","Mário A. T. Figueiredo"],"year":2025,"published":"2025-09-10","abstract":"This paper extends high-probability Learn-Then-Test risk control to covariate shift by importance-weighting calibration losses. It targets finite-sample control of user-specified risks when calibration and deployment distributions differ and validates the method on fraud detection and image classification. The method is a transferable candidate for calibrating RAG stopping policies when the query distribution shifts.","venue":"Fourteenth Symposium on Conformal and Probabilistic Prediction with Applications"}
{"title":"Score-Based Diffusion Priors for Adaptive Conformal Inference under Distribution Shift","url":"https://proceedings.mlr.press/v337/jiang26a.html","authors":["Xiangyu Jiang"],"year":2026,"abstract":"DiffConf develops adaptive conformal inference for temporal and covariate distribution shifts using score-based diffusion priors plus online recalibration. It provides finite-sample marginal coverage guarantees while the data distribution evolves and analyzes additional coverage behavior under drift assumptions. This is transfer evidence for online risk calibration in a non-stationary RAG controller.","venue":"UAI 2026"}
{"title":"Risk Controlled Image Retrieval","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2307.07336","arxiv_id":"2307.07336","doi":"10.1609/aaai.v39i26.34931","authors":["Kaiwen Cai","Chris Xiaoxuan Lu","Xingyu Zhao","Wei Huang","Xiaowei Huang"],"year":2025,"published":"2025-04-11","abstract":"RCIR constructs adaptive retrieval sets with a specified coverage guarantee, rather than returning a fixed-size set with only heuristic uncertainty. The guarantee controls the probability that a retrieval set contains a true nearest neighbor and is agnostic to the underlying retrieval model and data distribution. Although developed for image retrieval, it is transfer evidence for explicit risk-controlled evidence-set sizing.","venue":"AAAI 2025"}
{"title":"On Optimum Recognition Error and Reject Tradeoff","url":"https://doi.org/10.1109/TIT.1970.1054406","doi":"10.1109/TIT.1970.1054406","authors":["C. K. Chow"],"year":1970,"published":"1970-01-31","abstract":"Chow formalizes the trade-off between prediction error and rejecting uncertain cases and derives an optimum rejection rule. The paper establishes the classical reject-option foundation on which modern selective prediction, abstention, and risk-coverage approaches build.","venue":"IEEE Transactions on Information Theory"}
{"title":"A Taxonomy of Time in Databases","url":"https://www2.cs.arizona.edu/~rts/pubs/SIGMOD85.pdf","authors":["Richard Snodgrass","Ilsoo Ahn"],"year":1985,"abstract":"This foundational temporal-database paper introduces a taxonomy of distinct time dimensions for representing time-varying facts and clarifies previously inconsistent terminology. It classifies database capabilities according to which temporal dimensions they represent and argues that multiple time dimensions are needed to capture temporal behavior accurately. These semantics underpin later distinctions between evidence validity and database-record history.","venue":"ACM SIGMOD 1985"}
{"title":"Semantics of time-varying information","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/0306-4379(96)00017-8","doi":"10.1016/0306-4379(96)00017-8","authors":["Christian S. Jensen","Richard T. Snodgrass"],"year":1996,"abstract":"The paper systematically develops semantics for temporal database facts, including valid-time and transaction-time dimensions and their combination in bitemporal relations. It also discusses multiple transaction times created when facts are replicated or transferred between databases. These distinctions are directly transferable to version-aware evidence provenance where factual validity and system-observation history must not be conflated.","venue":"Information Systems"}
{"title":"Time-based language models","url":"https://dblp.org/rec/conf/cikm/LiC03","authors":["Xiaoyan Li","W. Bruce Croft"],"year":2003,"abstract":"This early temporal information-retrieval work identifies queries that favor recent documents and incorporates time into query-likelihood and relevance language models. Experiments compare the resulting temporal models with heuristic recency ranking. It is foundational temporal-IR work, while its recency formulation by itself does not provide document-version or effective-time validity semantics.","venue":"CIKM 2003"}
{"title":"Survey of Temporal Information Retrieval and Related Applications","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/2619088","doi":"10.1145/2619088","authors":["Ricardo Campos","Gaël Dias","Alípio Mário Jorge","Adam Jatowt"],"year":2014,"abstract":"This survey organizes the temporal information-retrieval literature and reviews methods that exploit temporal information in documents and queries. It distinguishes multiple temporal search needs, summarizes temporal query analysis and retrieval models, and discusses open problems in end-to-end temporal retrieval. The survey provides transfer context for separating ordinary recency ranking from richer temporal evidence requirements.","venue":"ACM Computing Surveys"}
